{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n7-day store mining (2026-08-07..14) found 33 FORCE_CLOSE audit records, ~25 of them ADMINISTRATIVE closes — duplicate, obsolete, wontfix, stale-premise — where completing the described work is not the intent, so the completion verifier can never PASS them and operators reach for --force. A narrow attested-disposition path exists but is doubly scoped down: (a) class set — `close_disposition.DISPOSITION_CLASSES` = {duplicate, not_a_bug, escalated} only; (b) ticket type — close_precheck.py:64 gates the whole path to `ticket_type == \"bug\"`, so an obsolete task/story/epic has NO truthful exit today. And the completion-FAIL message never mentions the path that does exist: \"it offered only 'complete the work' or '[operator-attested]' — attesting would have been a FALSE claim\" (9b70). This is the single largest FORCE_CLOSE class.\n\n## Fix (extends the EXISTING --class mechanism; no new flag)\n1. Vocabulary: add obsolete (premise no longer holds; reason required), superseded (replaced by a named live ticket), wontfix (deliberate decision; reason required) to the single-sourced `CLOSE_CLASSES` (txn.py:168). Enumerated co-edits, each single-sourced or drift-guarded: common.schema.json#/$defs/close_class enum+description (line 30; kept in the same order), ticket_state.schema.json close_class reference/description (:196-198), `close_disposition.DISPOSITION_CLASSES` AND `close_precheck._NON_COMPLETION_BUG_CLASSES` (two co-equal frozensets — BOTH gain the three classes, and their equality drift-guard tests/unit/test_close_disposition_attestation_738a.py:26 is updated with them), and the `transition.py` parser (`--class`/`--class=` — the existing flag gains the new values; there is NO new --disposition flag).\n2. Ticket-type widening: the disposition path (close_precheck.py:64) accepts non-bug tickets for the administrative classes {duplicate, obsolete, superseded, wontfix} — an obsolete task/story/epic closes truthfully. not_a_bug/escalated stay bug-only (they are bug vocabulary). Completion verification is skipped exactly as for today's bug dispositions; the plan-review claim gate is untouched.\n3. Reason payload: a NEW `close_reason` field on the close event + ticket_state.schema.json (NOT `force_close_reason`, which is the --force bypass channel and stays as-is). Required for obsolete/wontfix (refusal when missing); optional elsewhere. superseded/duplicate require a live replacement ticket resolved via the existing `find_replacement` (close_disposition.py:47).\n4. Attestation for reason-only classes: `close_disposition.verdict()` (:92) today returns None unless find_replacement resolves a live ticket, and `decorate_manifest` (:114) names that replacement in the signed bytes — so obsolete/wontfix as-written would close UNSIGNED (the 738a trap). Extend: for reason-only classes the verdict is minted from (class + close_reason) and decorate_manifest signs a `disposition: <class> reason: <text>` line instead of a replacement line; replacement-bearing classes are unchanged.\n5. Discoverability: the completion-FAIL / close-refusal message enumerates the disposition path when it applies — alongside \"complete the work\" and the external-evidence-only attestation path, name `transition <id> in_progress closed --class <class>` with the applicable class list. The 9b70 incident is the RED oracle: a wontfix-shaped ticket's failure message must present a truthful exit.\n6. Migration: existing FORCE_CLOSE records stay as-is (permanent audit); new administrative closes should have no reason to use --force.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] The three new classes appear in every single-sourced consumer — txn.CLOSE_CLASSES, common.schema.json + ticket_state.schema.json enums/descriptions, close_disposition.DISPOSITION_CLASSES, close_precheck._NON_COMPLETION_BUG_CLASSES — with the 738a equality drift-guard updated; a class present in one set but not the other is RED.\n- [ ] Administrative classes {duplicate, obsolete, superseded, wontfix} close non-bug tickets (task/story/epic fixture each) without completion verification; not_a_bug/escalated remain bug-only (refusal test).\n- [ ] obsolete/wontfix demand a close_reason (missing = refusal naming the requirement); superseded/duplicate demand a live replacement link (find_replacement); each records its payload in the close event (store-event test) — close_reason is a new field distinct from force_close_reason.\n- [ ] A reason-only disposition close mints a SIGNED disposition attestation whose manifest carries the class+reason line; replacement-bearing closes keep the replacement line; unsigned close for a valid disposition is RED.\n- [ ] The close-gate FAIL/refusal message enumerates the --class disposition path with applicable classes; message test asserts the 9b70 wontfix scenario is offered a truthful exit; --force on an administrative-shaped close hints the disposition alternative.\n- [ ] docs (ticket-model / user-guide) document the widened path; `make lint`/`make typecheck` clean; lands via Gerrit (LLM-Review +1, Verified +1)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786745247642331001,"uuid":"d5492dbd-59bc-43c9-b1c3-b7b0b33ba8e0"}